Chapter 10: "The Kingdom of the Lion King"

Chapter 10, Part 36: "The Great Fool"

—Krusch Karsten's heart had become a crucible swirling with impatience and self-reproach.

She was not aware that it was an impulse enlarged by external interference. But even if she had been aware, the dark stagnation within Krusch would not have changed.

Enlargement did not mean that something that had not existed was implanted. It was simply that something that should have grown over time had its time skipped and was fattened instead.

In other words, that "regret" was a sin that had been steadily growing within Krusch.

"—"

Still holding that untamable impulse, Krusch let out a soundless gnashing of teeth.

There was a click as her teeth meshed together inside her mouth. Despite that, no sound was produced outside her closed mouth—not even inside her own skull.

A world in which something that should exist has been removed brings an intensely uncomfortable sense of wrongness. And the opponent was terrifyingly precise in wedging the tip of a blade into the gaps of that discomfort.

"—"

She spun the knight's sword in her hand and struck away a beast-fang-like slash coming from the side. Silence.

Just like that, a stopped-in-place exchange of cuts began—one bout, two bouts, ten bouts—steel colliding with steel, and with the impact, fiery sparks bloomed countless times in midair. In silence.

Silence, silence, silence piled up. —No breathing, no heartbeat, no footsteps as they stepped in, no clashing of swords, not even the tongue-clicking of the belligerent-faced enemy. No sound could be heard at all.

That was the sudden abnormal battle that had attacked Krusch while she was moving alone within the castle.

"—"

—The reason Krusch had been able to respond to that abnormality was none other than the blessing of the "Dragon's Eye."

Thanks to this eye, which could distinguish even the fine dust drifting in the air while its power was released, Krusch was able to instantly defend against the "silent" surprise attack from behind. After defending, she realized. —If this enemy was the culprit who had taken Ferris, then his current predicament could have been prevented.

If Krusch had been able to master this "Dragon's Eye," the current situation could have been avoided.

"If so, then Ferris even now..."

Part of the regret welling up spilled from her lips, and she was startled by the fact that it became sound.

In that instant, catching up with her slightly disturbed footwork, the opponent's strike—swinging a great blade, something like a single-edged shears—shallowly cut Krusch's left shoulder as she failed to dodge in time.

"Ku..."

"You're slow, Krusch Karsten. You gotta look at me properly!"

Drawing his face close and speaking as if mocking her was a short man with blue hair and a small build.

The man, shorter than Krusch, handled the single-edged shears that seemed as long as his own height with ease, bounding freely through the narrow passage, and was holding his own against Krusch, who possessed the "Dragon's Eye."

The enemy's aim, his intentions, the various questions that instantly arose—she opened her mouth to sharply demand them—

"—"

Once again, Krusch's voice did not become sound.

The surrounding sounds that had returned for an instant were cut off again, and Krusch returned to an incomplete world with part of the finished painting scraped away. This had already been repeated over and over and over again.

If it were merely a lack of sound, she could rely on the vision of the "Dragon's Eye" and manage things a little better. But when the vanished sound was restored irregularly, and information of sound was suddenly brought down like a torrential rain after silence, continuing to fight while completely ignoring it was impossible for her current self.

"There—there—there—there—there—raa!"

Sound comes, doesn't come, comes, doesn't come, comes, doesn't come, comes. —It repeated that.

There were sword clashes that made sound, destruction that made no sound, failures that made sound, effective blows that made no sound, and a part of her consciousness was constantly robbed by the selection of information, so a hundred could not reside in the tip of her sword.

As a result, the wounds she received increased, she could not reach any chance to counterattack at all, and Krusch was forced into her first hard fight and disadvantage since acquiring the "Dragon's Eye."

"This is—"

The thin voice that spilled out miserably had its continuation erased by the opponent's power and did not become sound. Was it "This shouldn't be happening," or was it "At this rate"?

That was something neither the enemy nor, above all, Krusch—herself—understood.

"You sure are a stubborn woman! You're gonna lose to me and bawl your eyes out anyway, so don't make me waste time and just hurry up and die! You're annoying, everything about you!"

The man swung the single-edged shears in irritation, mercilessly cutting up the surrounding floor and walls.

The man's voice had sound; his rampage had no sound. The dissonance played by that fact stirred Krusch's heart. It was so mismatched, so unbearable. —Including herself.

"Ferris—"

"—Noooope."

The question of where he had been taken was erased, and the soundless exchange of sword strikes resumed.

She struck away, struck back, and knocked down the swung single-edged shears, but she could measure the sensation only through something other than response. Even while doing so, the man clicked his tongue irritably, "Tch," and—

"Annoying, annoying, you guys are annoying, endlessly annoying, shut up, annoying, don't breathe, annoying, don't blink, annoying, don't let your hair sway, annoying, don't stomp your feet, annoying, don't swing your sword, annoying, don't beat your heart, annoying, don't feel anything, annoying, your life is annoying."

"—"

"Don't go grinning like that, annoying! Don't make that motherly face, annoying! Don't be born, don't be alive, annoying, annoying, annoying!!"

The man's eyes were bloodshot, and with a resentment of anger unrelated to Krusch, he raged violently through the silence.

While handling that beast-like storm of blades with the power of the "Dragon's Eye," Krusch bit her navel in frustration as she was bathed in slashes of malice she had no recollection of.

Hostility, ill will, even murderous intent—that was all fine. —If it were directed at her.

But because it was not even that, because she was not even worth that, she—

And Ferris—

"—"

It was the moment when her heart was about to go blank from that rushing torrent of emotion and its explosion. —An unexpected blue light brushed simultaneously across Krusch and the man as they locked blades in silence.

"—" "...Hah?"

The fear of what the enemy would do next vanished when the man before her showed the same question.

The light just now had been unexpected for the man too; the surprise was mutual. And the same was true of the voice that came immediately after from beneath the feet of the two, who were glaring at each other at a distance where their breath mingled.

What she heard was a scream from far away, as if vomiting blood, filled with soul—

"—Don't die, idiot!! What are you thinking, don't mess around!!"

"—"

The moment she heard that familiar voice, one her ears knew well, a sensation ran across her entire body.

What arose was relief, then immediately a sense of mission, and belatedly anxiety and fear—she forced down all of it that surged up, and with all her strength Krusch thrust the man before her away. Aiming at the man who instinctively leapt back from that motion, her sword flashed—a Hundred-Man Single Slash was released.

As the sword wind with no place to escape mowed down the passage, the man, clicking his tongue, "Tch!", swung the single-edged shears, sliced through the ceiling, and escaped upward. He had avoided it. —But a gap had been created.

"Ferris—!"

Turning the sword she had swung down toward the floor, she hammered a fierce chain of strikes into it.

The tip of the sword became wind, carving star-like cuts into the white stone. —There, the ceiling directly above was cut, and the man who had vanished from her sight dropped a vertical split strike. In silence.

She received that strike with the sword raised above her head, and the man's red eyes and the "Dragon's Eye" crossed.

"Don't get in my—"

The back half of "way" was cut away, but it did not matter. The result was the same.

Her lifted leg powerfully stamped the floor, and immediately after, the foothold with cuts in it collapsed, and Krusch and the man, still locked blade-to-blade, fell into the sliced-open underground. In silence.

"—"

—The place they fell into was a castle underground space far larger than expected.

The traces of the "Melancholy Demon" having rampaged remained strongly even underground, and in the vast space that could serve as both a ballroom for a night banquet and a training ground for drills, there were a few isolated figures—among them Ferris, his arms pinned to the wall, and Subaru, lying in a pool of blood.

"—"

—In that instant, she could not think of any way to express the emotions that swelled up other than in red and black.

The storm of emotions that explosively surged up blew all rational thought and judgment far away. —And in that gap, she was caught off guard, taking a direct kick from the man as he spun. She caught it with a hastily raised knee, but her falling trajectory was forcibly bent, and she was sent flying toward the wall.

Planting her extended foot against the wall, she let the sole slide to soften the impact of the collision, and landed.

Looking straight ahead, the man with the single-edged shears stood on the opposite side of the circular space, and between him and her, in the center of the room, were a woman in a black dress and, for some reason, the "White Sheep" holding hands with her. The fallen Subaru was on the left, and Ferris, pinned to the wall, was on the right. —The situation was all too unclear.

Perhaps feeling the same confusion as Krusch, the man struck the floor hard with his single-edged shears and—

"Heeey, what the hell's going on, Belaris! Why'd the 'Detour' come undone!"

"Reborn! Coming right away once the 'Detour' came undone... could it be that you want me that much? You like me? I like you too!"

"You're a woman who can't hold a conversation! Shut up and answer what you're asked!"

"If I shut up, I can't answer either, right? A careless Reborn needs a proper girl with him. For example... yes! Like this Melty!"

"Melty...?"

Whether she had any intention of answering the question or not, the woman in the dress said that and grabbed the "White Sheep" by the shoulder, pushing her forward toward the man. Sandwiched between the two, who had called each other Reborn and Belaris, the "White Sheep" widened her eyes slightly, but her expression immediately became a cold smile.

And then—

"Nice to meet you, Reborn. I am Melty, one of the 'Beloved Children' chosen by Mama."

"What kind of 'Beloved Child' is that? Belaris, you got fooled so easily..."

"—Mama, thank you always. I love you most in the world."

At those low, spun words from the "White Sheep," Reborn drew in his breath, and anger vanished from his expression. What slipped in instead was a strong emotion of fear, and shame at himself for feeling it.

As she had heard, the relationship between the "Beloved Children" and their mother was cultivated in a distorted way.

She would admit she had thoughts about that mistaken way of spinning bonds. —But that did not give Krusch, whose thoughts were painted over by red and black emotions, any room to show them consideration.

She kicked off the floor. The enemy was in front, Reborn beyond, Belaris nearer, and the "White Sheep" as well—

"Melty is our comrade! I think she and Reborn would make a good match, so I ship it!"

Turning her back to Krusch, Belaris wore a full-faced smile at Reborn. —Fine, that was sincere.

Shaking off the arm on her shoulder, the "White Sheep" spun around and faced Krusch. In her hand, she extended a short cylindrical tool and gripped it as a single striking staff.

Then the "White Sheep," with a resolute will in her yellow eyes—

"—I am one of the 'Beloved Children,' your enemy, Miss 'Sui-Rei'!"

Saying that, she readied the striking staff with a movement that showed training, pointed its tip at the approaching Krusch, and showed a stance of standing in her way. However—that was bad.

"—A wind of lies is blowing, Melty."

"—Yes."

In that instant, the "White Sheep"—Melty—twisted her waist and turned around, and matching Krusch's step, Krusch's flash and her staff strike were unleashed in a diagonal cross.

It headed straight for Belaris, who stood still and defenseless—

"Oh my?"

The overlapping blow of sword wind and staff strike blew the woman in the dress away head-on.

△▼△▼△▼△

—The "Blessing of the Wind-Seer" discerned lies and truth and saw through the falsity of Melty's words.

If her words were a lie, then her position itself must have been held with some intention. In fact, matching that, Melty and Krusch together dealt a painful blow to Belaris.

"—But I was surprised."

There was no lie in her usual words and behavior, but her manner was hesitant—or rather cowardly—and she had seemed to fear interacting with people. —However, that assessment had been wrong.

Melty was brave, and a person with a strong, settled core. Far more than herself.

And on top of that—

"Next is your turn, my lord."

She directed her sword spirit toward the blown-away and fallen Belaris, and toward Reborn standing beyond her. Reborn, carrying the single-edged shears on his shoulder and wearing a dangerous look in his eyes, clicked his tongue and said—

"You idiots, don't say I didn't warn you."

That was a statement that did not quite make sense as anger over a comrade being taken down. But before confirming its true meaning, before beginning the next fight—

"—Lady Krusch! Before the enemy, Subaru-kun!"

The call made her start, and Krusch looked at Ferris, pinned to the wall.

What skewered both his arms above his head was one half of Reborn's single-edged shears. Yet even in that pitiful state, hung up with his arms pierced, what Ferris shouted about was not himself.

"Natsuki, Subaru, sama..."

Yes, Subaru was fallen. She should tend to him—no, should she prioritize eliminating the enemy? In the first place, what could she do by rushing over? What was needed was Ferris's healing. Then the order should obviously be Ferris's release first. Why could she not even make such an obvious judgment? Foolish, slow to understand, and far too insufficient. How could someone like this ever have been called the "War Maiden"—

"—!"

A fierce self-loathing, a dark emotion, suddenly exploded within her chest.

She felt she would drop her sword. Her knees felt like trembling. A sob seemed about to spill. What on earth had suddenly happened? For some reason, all at once, inside her head, deep in her chest, the important part of her heart felt as if it would break.

"Love always comes with obstacles. —Being split into enemies and allies makes it burn, too."

She heard a voice murmur that.

The woman lying on her back on the ground, hands clasped before her chest—Belaris—spoke in a voice roughened by the pain of her wounds, colored by passion for something greater than pain.

And that voice awakened the dark stagnation within Krusch in a form that could no longer be suppressed.

—I want to die. Rather, I want to disappear entirely—a violent emotion unbefitting Krusch Karsten, beginning to curse herself.

"That's why I told you!"

"—!"

The blade of Reborn was hammered down at Krusch, who was about to buckle at the knees, about to have her heart give in, crushed by the impulse. The sword she reflexively raised cut into that trajectory, but her cowardly stance had no strength to defend, and she was blown straight sideways by the impact. In silence.

"This, the 'Collapse' is getting stronger... Subaru-sa—"

Melty's words, as she cut in between Krusch and the enemy to protect her, vanished at the end. The discomfort of the silence inserted into the gap of her call was enough to confuse Melty, seeing it for the first time.

And mercilessly taking advantage of that confusion, the blade skewered the brave girl's abdomen.

"—"

Melty crumpled powerlessly on the spot, and the striking staff rolled from her hand. In silence.

Blood burst out, and Melty's face twisted in pain as she cried out. In silence.

Beside the fallen Melty, Reborn raised the single-edged shears. In silence.

"—"

She threw herself recklessly in front of the finishing blade as it swung down. In silence.

Tracing the trajectory shown by the "Dragon's Eye," she swung her sword to protect her comrade. In silence.

The sword flashes bounded freely, and the exchange with the irritated-looking Reborn continued. In silence.

"—"

Silence, silence, silence. Focus, focus, focus. Undisturbed, undisturbed, undisturbed. Self-mastery, self-mastery, self-mastery—.

"—"

There was a voice tormenting and blaming her. Once again, a voice cursing that "you" had made a mistake.

She had heard Ferris's voice and gone underground to save him. But before Krusch arrived, Melty had slipped into the bosom of the "Beloved Child" and gained trust. It must have been an action within some plan, with a picture of success in their own way visible.

Then Krusch's unforeseen intervention occurred, and Melty was forced to change course.

The result was her, now fallen on the ground. Subaru, lying some distance away. Ferris, pinned to the wall. And herself, fighting only on the defensive.

"—"

If she had not come, would things have gone well? These past two months, the McMahon residence, the water gate city of Pristella—everything Krusch Karsten had failed at.

"—"

The dark stagnation spread wide, and even in the middle of battle, a sense of helplessness covered her heart.

Eventually, when it covered her whole body, she would no longer be able to hold her sword. Before that, it was more likely that she would be abandoned by the power of the "Dragon's Eye" and have her life cut off by that blade.

If there, in the end, the only difference was whether it came late or soon—

"—Lift your head! Krusch Karsten!!"

In that instant, a desperate voice leapt into the silent world and set her nearly stopped arm back into motion.

She struck it away. Clinging to life. She made it be struck away. Without any grace. What had made her do that?

That was—

"There's still time! No one has ended yet! I won't let anyone end!"

The one who roared with her mouth wide open like that was Ferris, with anger dwelling in his yellow eyes.

For some reason, his voice reached her. It shook her eardrums. That made her swing her sword, but the dark stagnation soaking her whole body also gained momentum from that voice.

"—"

It was no one else. He was the one she could least face. She had wounded him with words she should never have said, left the sin in place with no way to atone, and was about to be crushed by an insoluble guilt.

To such a weak, foolish self, Ferris raised his voice.

"Subaru-kun and 'White Sheep'-chan are fighting too! Fight without giving up! Fight... please! Let me fight too!"

Supported by the voice poured over her, she somehow barely resisted the violent blade being struck at her. While resisting, a voiceless voice caught in the back of her throat at Ferris's appeal.

Let me fight, Ferris had said. —The true meaning of that reached her clearly.

And her heart immediately screamed that doing so was impossible.

"It's not impossible. It's not impossible at all. You can do it. —If it's you, Lady Krusch!"

Perhaps he had read the weakness from her expression or eyes; it was encouragement like scooping up a heart about to break.

Surely, if it had been anyone but herself, it might have been a cheer to rouse the soul. And yet, the helplessness of being unable to meet the expectations placed on her instead robbed her of strength.

The qualification to answer Ferris's straightforward loyal love belonged only to Krusch Karsten—

"—I've known for ages! Your memories haven't come back, have they!?"

—In that instant, her breathing stopped.

△▼△▼△▼△

He said it. He had finally said it.

Even though he knew that if he said this, he would make her show the face he least wanted to see.

"—"

Krusch, her eyes now two colors—amber and gold—wide open, stared at him in shock. Suppressing the urge to look away from that gaze, Felix twisted his body.

Not to regain his freedom. But to project his voice from his belly.

"I knew! How long do you think I've been with you!? I saw through all of it from the very beginning!"

A lie. —He had noticed because of these past two months.

At first, her figure, grieving from the heart over Felix's decision, had been so painful that he could not think properly about anything. It was only after time had passed and he had calmed down, and after the two months of acting together with the "Six-Tongued," that he had been able to hold this doubt.

"But I'm a cowardly, sneaky weakling, so I was afraid and couldn't confirm it."

Surely that too was one of the countless sins Felix had committed.

"—"

While listening to Felix's appeal, Krusch struck away the single-edged shears.

He could not hear any reply from her. Rather, for some reason, none of the sounds of the battle between Krusch and the shears man reached Felix's ears at all. Silence.

But that his own voice reached her was clear from Krusch's face, which grew cloudier the more he spoke.

He did not want to hurt her. He had never once thought of driving her into a corner. —But if that drastic treatment was necessary, then even sticking a finger into the wound had to be accepted as treatment.

Otherwise, he could not save the life of the great fool who had taken the gamble of believing in a Felix like this, the great fool for whom there was no medicine and no magic to apply, now on the verge of slipping away.

"You can't do it, you can't do it—your head's in complete chaos, right? You think it's impossible for you, and everything before your eyes is pitch black. —But I'll correct one thing in your thinking, Krusch Karsten."

That was a phrase Krusch liked to use when exchanging words with others.

Deliberately saying it, Felix fixed his gaze on the wavering amber and gold—

"—You can do it. If it's you and me, we can do it."

Ahh, what a foolish appeal to emotion, what an unreasonable fatalism.

No, no, he really hated it. He did not want to look directly at it. He did not want to acknowledge it. He could not accept it.

This was exactly the same as what a certain great fool was doing. —No different at all from the great fool who believed and expected on his own, and used that trust as a shield to threaten.

But it could not be helped. There was no other move.

Because that very great fool was standing on his own at the edge of a cliff where every second counted—there was no choice but to adopt as-is the great fool's method that had lit a fire in Felix's heart.

"—Lady Krusch!"

At Felix's appeal, at his gaze, Krusch's expression distorted greatly.

Her lips moved, spinning words that became no sound, trying to say it. —I can't.

"—You can!"

He could not hear it. Therefore it had not been said. He pushed through and pierced with that absurd argument.

Grief, anger, longing—he would deal with all of it later. Before life, everything else was trivial.

You can't? What's the reason? —Because you're not the Krusch Karsten you once were?

"You're not the you from sometime! I'm asking the Lady Krusch who is here, now!"

"—"

"Let's save it! You and me! This kingdom that His Highness loved!!"

At that voice, the two-colored eyes looked straight at Felix. —In those pupils, a fire was lit.

The white blade was turned toward the heavens, and in the silent world, the raging wind became a blade and took shape.

That was the synonym of the "War Maiden" Krusch Karsten—the flash of salvation that had once mowed down the swarm of "Great Rabbits" attacking her domain, changed their course, and saved many of her people.

—The Hundred-Man Single Slash, poured with all her strength, was released into the underground of Kugelschreiber Castle.

△▼△▼△▼△

—Until the very last moment of swinging the sword through, hesitation continued to drag behind her.

"—"

Her breath did not become sound, her voice did not become sound, and the sword flash and its result also did not become sound.

Still, she had witnessed the result of the released blade. That was at least her duty, even while forgetting Krusch Karsten and trying to impersonate the "War Maiden."

—The dark stagnation that tormented and cursed her was still writhing within her chest now.

It still made Krusch's hands and feet heavy. Negative emotions would grow without limit, and surely by now they should have covered all of Krusch.

However—

"Damn bastards! You and that guy too, none of you are sane!"

Amid the complete suppression of footsteps, sword flashes, and environmental sounds, Reborn's voice rang out.

While cutting away the single-edged shears in his hand, she felt keenly that she had done something for which there was no reply. —Would the Krusch Karsten of old have done this?

She did not know. And now, that had no meaning.

The one who had been expected by Ferris, believed in, and answered was this Krusch Karsten.

And that self-recognition became a torrent of positive emotion, filling Krusch's mind and body with vitality enough not to lose to the dark stagnation in her chest, and making her resist Reborn as well.

And so, while Krusch drew the enemy's attention—

"Really, you're such a huge idiot that maybe Subaru-kun should just die once?"

"Hey hey, don't say something like that even as a joke. I don't even want to think about dying."

Beyond Reborn, with whom she crossed steel, she could hear the voices of the two trading barbs.

The boy who had barely been scooped back from the verge of death, and the "Blue" who had scooped up his life, were leaning close to each other atop the pool of blood as if embracing.

As if Ferris were embracing Subaru, who had one knee raised, from behind. —But that it was not an embrace of affection was obvious at a glance from their reluctant expressions.

In fact, it had to be done. —Ferris's healing magic required touching the other person's body in order to activate.

"Rather, this is way more reckless on your part."

"It's mutual, isn't it?"

So saying, Subaru, with a knife growing from his side, and Ferris, whose arms had been cut off by Krusch from the elbows down and left on the wall, snorted through his nose.

—It was the quarrel of two great fools, the kind of thing one would naturally call insane.
